  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The equivalence class $[5]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $5$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[5] & =\\{x\\in Z\\,:\\,x∼5\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,:\\,7\\,|\\,(x-5)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,:\\,x=5+7k,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-9,-2,5,12,19,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $12$ and $-2$ are in $[5],$ while $-4$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
